      "inputTokens": 185,
      "outputTokens": 1332,
      "costUsd": 0.012080500000000001,
      "pronunciationText": "Here's how these numbers get used. A pet insurer charges three hundred dollars a year to cover a cat. In a year, there's a zero point zero two chance of paying an eight thousand dollar claim, a zero point zero five chance of paying a one thousand dollar claim, and otherwise nothing. The expected payout is eight thousand times zero point zero two, plus one thousand times zero point zero five, which is two hundred and ten dollars. On the beam, that small weight far out at eight thousand does most of the pulling. So in the long run, the premium beats the expected claims by ninety dollars a policy, before the insurer's other costs. But the standard deviation of the payout is about one thousand one hundred and thirty-four dollars. One policy swings far more than ninety dollars either way. That's why insurers need many independent policies, so the long-run average takes over."
    },
